,\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L61\",null,{\"src\":\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/wtf-are-jwts/neon-jwt-1-1024x576-0397aca7.jpg\",\"isDark\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/wtf-are-jwts/neon-jwt-1-1024x576-0397aca7.jpg\",\"alt\":\"Image\"}]}]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"We recently launched \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/blog/introducing-neon-authorize\",\"children\":\"Neon RLS\"}],\" to simplify Postgres authorization with row-level security.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"If you’ve been reading our posts, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/docs/guides/neon-authorize-tutorial\",\"children\":\"tutorials\"}],\", or \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/neondatabase-labs/clerk-nextjs-neon-authorize\",\"children\":\"READMEs\"}],\" on the subject, you’ll have come across three little letters: JWT. This whole world seems to run on JWTs–so what are they?\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"JWTs, or JSON Web Tokens to give them their full titles, or JOTs to give their weird pronunciation, are standardized, compact, self-contained tokens for transmitting information. They are JSON objects that are digitally signed so each party can trust and verify the information.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Every day, when you use the web, you use JWTs frictionlessly. The same goes for Neon RLS. Unless you want to go full custom, your auth provider–Clerk, Auth0, Stack Auth, and so on–will take care of this for you. But if you want to implement Neon RLS in your application, it doesn’t hurt to understand what is happening under the hood with the JWTs flying around. That’s what we’re going to do here.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"the-history-of-the-jwt\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"The History of the JWT\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#the-history-of-the-jwt\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to The History of the JWT\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeLinecap\":\"square\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"strokeWidth\":1.4,\"d\":\"m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75\"}]}]}]]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"JSON Web Tokens emerged in the early 2010s as part of a broader evolution in web authentication.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Before JWTs, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://roadmap.sh/guides/session-based-authentication\",\"children\":\"session-based authentication\"}],\" was the norm–servers would create a session ID, store it in a database, and send it to the client as a cookie. While this worked, it had scaling limitations: every authentication request needed a database lookup, and session state had to be shared across multiple servers.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The need for a more efficient, stateless authentication method grew alongside the rise of mobile apps, single-page applications (SPAs), and distributed systems. Traditional session-based auth wasn’t cutting it anymore. Developers needed something that could work across domains, platforms, and services without constant database hits.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Enter JWTs. In May 2015, JWT was standardized as \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7519\",\"children\":\"RFC 7519\"}],\" by the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF). The standard defined \",\"$L62\",\" Their self-contained nature made JWTs revolutionary. All the necessary information is in the token, cryptographically signed to ensure authenticity.\"]}],\"\\n\",\"$L63\",\"\\n\",\"$L64\",\"\\n\",\"$L65\",\"\\n\",\"$L66\",\"\\n\",\"$L67\",\"\\n\",\"$L68\",\"\\n\",\"$L69\",\"\\n\",\"$L6a\",\"\\n\",\"$L6b\",\"\\n\",\"$L6c\",\"\\n\",\"$L6d\",\"\\n\",\"$L6e\",\"\\n\",\"$L6f\",\"\\n\",\"$L70\",\"\\n\",\"$L71\",\"\\n\",\"$L72\",\"\\n\",\"$L73\",\"\\n\",\"$L74\",\"\\n\",\"$L75\",\"\\n\",\"$L76\",\"\\n\",\"$L77\",\"\\n\",\"$L78\",\"\\n\",\"$L79\",\"\\n\",\"$L7a\",\"\\n\",\"$L7b\",\"\\n\",\"$L7c\",\"\\n\",\"$L7d\",\"\\n\",\"$L7e\",\"\\n\",\"$L7f\",\"\\n\",\"$L80\",\"\\n\",\"$L81\",\"\\n\",\"$L82\",\"\\n\",\"$L83\",\"\\n\",\"$L84\",\"\\n\",\"$L85\",\"\\n\",\"$L86\",\"\\n\",\"$L87\",\"\\n\",\"$L88\",\"\\n\",\"$L89\",\"\\n\",\"$L8a\",\"\\n\",\"$L8b\",\"\\n\",\"$L8c\",\"\\n\",\"$L8d\",\"\\n\",\"$L8e\",\"\\n\",\"$L8f\",\"\\n\",\"$L90\",\"\\n\",\"$L91\",\"\\n\",\"$L92\",\"\\n\",\"$L93\",\"\\n\",\"$L94\",\"\\n\",\"$L95\",\"\\n\",\"$L96\",\"\\n\",\"$L97\",\"\\n\",\"$L98\",\"\\n\",\"$L99\",\"\\n\",\"$L9a\",\"\\n\",\"$L9b\",\"\\n\",\"$L9c\",\"\\n\",\"$L9d\",\"\\n\",\"$L9e\",\"\\n\",\"$L9f\",\"\\n\",\"$La0\",\"\\n\",\"$La1\",\"\\n\",\"$La2\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,":HL[\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/wtf-are-jwts/ad4nxcqdymntx8ekhac7skraibyy4tr41kh3rnljvtmbzbkn2nc7ft3iuu5jz9inptk44zrb0ndmkmu4qr6yqcnd9lqjzztejbq6drdtiiwbksslgadvzfks8oxb29s5whumznstg-ebfa648c.png\",\"image\"]\n:HL[\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/wtf-are-jwts/screenshot-2024-11-29-at-83349percente2percent80percentafam-1024x908-66444d48.png\",\"image\"]\n62:[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"“a compact, URL-safe means of representing claims to be transferred between two parties.”\"}]\n63:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The for